        "text": "Release-date verification limitation: CMS's official release listing verifies June 2026 publication on September 25, 2026, and earlier actual publication dates, but supplies no announced August 2026 publication date. December 15 in the specifications concerns an illustrative updated-report submission deadline for states, not public publication. The contract's resolutionDate 2026-12-15 is retained, but could not be verified from an official release calendar or announcement this run; that validation requirement remains unmet."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "For fetched rates [74,75,75,76,77,77,78,74,72,71,71,72], successive changes are [1,0,1,1,0,1,-4,-2,-1,0,1] percentage points. There are 11 changes, their sum is -2 and sum of squares is 26. Sample variance = (26 - (-2)^2/11)/(11-1) = 2.563636; sigma = 1.6011 percentage points. The one-month normal 80% half-width is 1.28 \u00d7 1.60113596 = 2.049454. For June to August, multiply by sqrt(2): half-width = 2.898366. Thus 72.0 \u00b1 2.898366 gives 69.101634\u201374.898366%, rounded to 69.1\u201374.9%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a zero-drift persistence model with twelve fetched original-period Arkansas prints and eleven successive changes. Level is 72.0%; momentum, one-off and policy adjustments are each zero. sigma = 1.6011 percentage points; 1.28*sigma = 2.0495, widened by sqrt(2) for two unobserved months to 2.8984. This is 1.414 times the one-month half-width. Final point is 72.0% and nominal 80% bounds are 69.1\u201374.9%, with calibration limited by rounded history and the small sample."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The upside risk is improved data matching or a renewal cohort resembling late 2025: a return to 75\u201378% would land above the interval. The downside risk is disrupted matching, a harder-to-renew cohort, or more renewals classified as form-based: a share below 69.1% would land outside the interval. Another regime shift comparable to the January-to-February decline could invalidate the normal persistence approximation."
